# 176 — Private database dump creation (SECURITY-FIX-DEPLOY-BACKUP-FILE-PERMISSIONS-1)

A database dump contains the entire clinical database. It is HIGHLY SENSITIVE.

## Non-negotiable rules

1. **Database backup files must be private from the moment of creation.**
   Post-creation `chmod` is NOT an acceptable primary security mechanism — the
   shell creates a redirect target (and `pg_dump -f` creates its file) under the
   caller's umask BEFORE a byte is written, so `pg_dump > file; chmod 0640 file`
   leaves the inode 0644 for the whole dump.
2. **Every dump is written through `scripts/lib/private-db-dump.sh`**
   (`dms_write_private_dump <dest> pg_dump ...`). It applies `umask 077` in a
   subshell scoped to the dump only (the umask never leaks to the rest of the
   deploy), writes `<dest>.partial` with bash `noclobber` (O_EXCL — refuses a
   pre-planted file or symlink), removes the partial on failure or empty output,
   then publishes it **unchanged at 0600** by atomic rename. There is
   deliberately NO chmod after the write: deploy runs as root inside a
   runtime-owned directory, where a path-based chmod could be redirected through
   a swapped-in symlink. Never re-implement this inline. Modes are literals in
   the helper, never variables — callers source the environment file around it.
3. **Backup directories are 2750** (`dms_prepare_private_backup_dir`): no access
   for "other", not even traverse. A directory carrying a DEFAULT ACL is refused,
   because a default ACL replaces the umask in the kernel's create-mode calculation.
4. **No deployment or backup helper may create a database dump with
   world-readable permissions, even transiently** — including temporary files,
   partial files and compressed variants.
5. **The storage-wide 2775/0664 normalization must PRUNE `storage/app/backups`**
   (`-path storage/app/backups -prune`) and set the tree to 2750/0640 directly.
   Widening and re-restricting afterwards is the same redirect-then-chmod window
   applied to every dump on the host at once.
6. **Changes to backup/deploy dump logic require permission-mode regression
   tests** that observe the mode AT CREATION (the probe stats its own stdout via
   `/proc/$BASHPID/fd/1` while running) — a final-mode assertion cannot see the
   defect. Suite: `tests/Feature/Deploy/PrivateDatabaseDumpCreationTest.php`.
7. **Production backup contents and credentials never appear in logs or
   deployment evidence.** Verify a backup with metadata (size, owner, mode) and
   the existing `foundation:backup-verify` gate — never `cat`, `head` or `grep`
   a dump. The password reaches pg_dump only as `PGPASSWORD` in that one
   command's environment, never as an argument.

## Approved modes

| Object | Mode | Owner |
|---|---|---|
| Dump while being written | 0600 | creator (root in deploy, runtime user in the scheduled backup) |
| Dump as published by the helper | 0600 | creator |
| Dump after deploy normalization | 0640 | runtime user : runtime group |
| Backup directory | 2750 | runtime user : runtime group |

The runtime group contains only the dedicated runtime identity; "other" — which
includes the co-tenant shared web account — has no access at any point.
